On Thu, 11 May 2006, GrettaCook@gmail.com wrote:

> The FAQ should be updated to reflect current standards.  Breaking out
> 2.5A and posting it separately, or somehow calling attention to it,
> also seems like a good idea.  One has the impression, when reading it,
> that one has an outdated version of a spoiler.

Then Section 2.5 of the general rgrn FAQ should contain a very brief
digest of the netiquette, pointing out only the most important aspects.
Something like:

  To ensure that your articles are read by the rgrn community, you should
  adhere to some basic standards of posting:
    * Quote the context which is necessary to understand your message
      (but not more). Signatures are nearly always irrelevant.
    * Include attributions of quoted material.
    * Don't top-post: Write your answers below any quoted text.
    * Choose meaningful subjects for new posts: "Help!" or "question"
      are bad, "difficulty of classes" or "How to cure lycantrophy?"
      are good.
  For a more thorough treatment of rgrn posting standards, read ***.

  Your best bet, as with most newsgroups, is to read the newsgroup for a
  while and get a feeling for the place before you post.


This seems to me to contain the most important points. Binaries, HTML
posts, overlong signatures and overlong lines come up so seldomly that
they do not need to be included in this short list.

